 a failure is observable"],"finding":"Acceptance-criteria / definition-of-done quality issues.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f607dd457c67f9209","impact":0.4,"is_primary":null,"location":"","norm_id":"ned06ebc801a522e3","priority":0.4,"reason":null,"recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Split compound criteria, replace subjective terms with observable outcomes, and state how each is verified.","tier":"DET","validity":1.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add a measured Linux enforcement probe (symmetric to the macOS three-probe table) demonstrating a bwrap-wrapped write outside the allow-list and `rm -rf <protected>/*` are DENIED and the target survives — do not rely on `shutil.which` presence alone to establish bwrap's write-deny.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.5548,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan text: macOS section presents a measured probe table ('Measured on this host ... rm -rf <protected>/* — DENIED — target survived'); the Linux section presents no analogous measurement, only 'bwrap is the committed Linux mechanism' and 'Without bwrap, Linux fails closed.'","The plan itself established (T1/T3 prior round) that an unverified write-deny claim for a namespace mechanism (unshare --mount without ro-remount) is a real defect — the same verification standard should apply to the bwrap bind-mount write-deny, since bwrap's read-only enforcement depends on binds being made ro (containers/bubblewrap issue #650 notes a bind can fail to be made read-only and the tool must fail rather than leave it writable).","Web-search corroboration: bubblewrap docs confirm the mechanism CAN deny writes (new mount namespace, explicit binds, ro-remount), so the capability is resolvable — the gap is verification-in-plan, not feasibility."],"finding":"The macOS sandbox-exec write-deny is verified with a measured three-probe table on-host, but the symmetric Linux bwrap write-deny (that a bwrap-wrapped `rm -rf <protected>/*` is actually DENIED and the target survives) is asserted as 'fails closed' / 'committed Linux mechanism' without the equivalent measured evidence. Prior art confirms bwrap's default new-mount-namespace + explicit-bind model can enforce this, but the plan leaves the load-bearing Linux enforcement claim unverified where it verified the macOS one.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f83ef1c6dadd73c2d","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"\"Linux mechanism: bubblewrap only\" section; contrasted with \"macOS mechanism: verified, not asserted\"","norm_id":"n1c008dabc7fa8fa1","priority":0.1452,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A bwrap invocation whose allow-list binds are mounted read-write (or whose read-only remount silently fails) would probe as 'present' and run, yet not deny the incident's `rm -rf` — reproducing on Linux the exact failure the plan guards against on macOS."],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"In the 'Linux mechanism' section, reproduce the macOS-style probe table on an ubuntu-latest host (or under the enforcement test): write-outside-allow-list DENIED, write-inside allowed, `rm -rf <protected>/*` DENIED, plus an assertion that the allow-list binds are read-write while everything else is unwritable — mirroring AC line for the enforcement test so bwrap's write-deny is observed, not just its presence.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.55,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"insufficient","cited_reference_accurate":"na","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"Confirm the allow-list explicitly covers mutmut's own runtime writes (its results cache/DB and any in-place source rewrites under the copied tree) so a legitimate `mutmut run` is not itself denied, and add a positive-path test that a normal mutmut run completes under the sandbox.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.501,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan allow-list: 'denying filesystem writes outside an explicit allow-list (scratch tree, pytest basetemp, the venv, /dev/null)'","mutmut run (line 583) executes and persists mutation results; the harness 'string-substitutes the artifact under test', implying in-place writes to the copied source tree during a run","The plan verifies the DENY case but does not state that the legitimate mutmut run's own writes (cache/DB + in-tree source rewrites) are confirmed inside the allow-list"],"finding":"The sandbox allow-list is enumerated as scratch tree, pytest basetemp, venv, and /dev/null, but mutmut run itself writes state (its mutation results cache/DB and in-place source mutations within the working tree) that may fall outside that list; if so, the legitimate mutmut run would be denied by the sandbox, breaking the gate rather than only blocking the destructive path.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f9799050cf21594f3","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria, first bullet (allow-list enumeration)","norm_id":"na25a5e1657311bfa","priority":0.099,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["mutmut run attempts to write its results DB or rewrite source in a path not covered by the scratch-tree subpath -> sandbox denies -> mutmut exits non-zero -> GateError('mutation-tool') on a legitimate run"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"State that the allow-list subpath is the copied source/scratch root that already contains mutmut's cache and the mutated sources, and add an acceptance probe asserting an unmutated mutmut run succeeds (not just that an out-of-list write is denied).","tier":"LLM","validity":0.375,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"na","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add a measured Linux probe (analogous to the macOS table) demonstrating the specific bwrap invocation DENIES a write/rm outside the allow-list and survives the target, and confirm bwrap is present (or install it) on the ubuntu-latest CI runner — or route the Linux mechanism to a SPIKE.","cohort":["T3"],"criteria":["T3"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.7812,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan quote: 'bwrap is the committed Linux mechanism' — asserted with no probe table analogous to the macOS 'DENIED — target survived' measurements","The macOS section is titled 'verified, not asserted' and lists measured probe results; the Linux section contains no equivalent measured deny result","Plan rejects the unshare fallback precisely because it 'does not deny writes without a read-only remount' (T1/T3) — the same write-deny mechanic for bwrap is left unverified","bwrap cannot be probed from the macOS review host; the CI matrix _build-and-test.yml:311 is [ubuntu-latest, macos-latest] but bwrap presence on ubuntu-latest is not confirmed — EVIDENCE CLASS: Unverified"],"finding":"The Linux bwrap mechanism is asserted as the committed sandbox but its write-deny enforcement is never measured, unlike the macOS sandbox-exec probes which the plan reports as measured on-host. No concrete bwrap flag composition (e.g., --ro-bind / --bind allow-list) is shown, and there is no confirmation bwrap is available on the ubuntu-latest runner named in Platform scope.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f1614dd53d16bf3d7","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Section 'Linux mechanism: bubblewrap only' and AC line 'Linux uses bwrap, probed at runtime via shutil.which'","norm_id":"nec9395fd2eec666d","priority":0.1188,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["On Linux CI (ubuntu-latest) the bwrap flag composition fails to actually deny writes outside the allow-list, reproducing the incident on the OS the incident-fix targets","bwrap is absent on ubuntu-latest by default, so every Linux run fails closed and the gate never actually exercises the sandbox in CI"],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Run the same three probes (write outside allow-list, write inside, rm -rf <protected>/*) under the concrete bwrap argv on a Linux host, record the result table in the plan as done for macOS, and state how bwrap is provisioned on ubuntu-latest (apt install bubblewrap in the workflow, or documented as required).","tier":"LLM","validity":0.45